body:
Multiple memory safety vulnerabilities in path construction and `/proc` parsing that could lead to heap/stack buffer overflows and out-of-bounds memory access.

## Heap buffer overflow — `rurienv.c` (`is_ruri_pid`)
`malloc(PATH_MAX)` + `buf[len] = '\0'` where `readlink` can return `len == PATH_MAX`, writing one byte past the allocation. Fixed by allocating `PATH_MAX + 1`.

## Stack buffer overflow — `chroot.c` (`check_binary`)
```c
// Before: combined length of two user-controlled inputs unchecked
char qemu_binary[PATH_MAX];
strcpy(qemu_binary, container->container_dir);
strcat(qemu_binary, container->qemu_path);

// After
if (snprintf(qemu_binary, sizeof(qemu_binary), "%s%s",
             container->container_dir, container->qemu_path) >= (int)sizeof(qemu_binary)) {
    ruri_error(...);
}
```

## Out-of-bounds read/write — `ps.c` (`getpid_name`, `getpid_stat`)
Inner parsing loops used unguarded indices `buf[k + i + 1]` (could exceed `buf[8192]`) and wrote to `name_buf[k]` without checking `k < sizeof(name_buf)`. Both destination buffers were also uninitialized. Added bounds checks on both source and destination, initialized buffers to `""`.

## Pervasive `sprintf` → `snprintf` — `chroot.c`, `rurienv.c`, `umount.c`, `config.c`, `ruri.c`
All `sprintf(buf, "%s/<suffix>", container_dir)` patterns where `buf` is `char[PATH_MAX]` and `container_dir` is user-controlled. Replaced throughout with `snprintf` + truncation checks.

## Missing null-termination after `readlink` — `config.c`
`readlink("/proc/self/exe", buf, PATH_MAX)` with a `PATH_MAX`-byte buffer; not null-terminated when path fills the buffer. Changed limit to `PATH_MAX - 1` with explicit `buf[len] = '\0'`.

## Wrong-array free in `extra_ro_mountpoint` loop — `umount.c`
Pre-existing bug: the ro-mountpoint unmount loop freed `extra_mountpoint[i]` instead of `extra_ro_mountpoint[i]`, leaking the ro-mountpoint strings.

